/* LLM Fundamentals — 66 questions */
window.QA_SECTION = {
  id: '01',
  slug: 'llm-fundamentals',
  title: 'LLM Fundamentals',
  blurb: 'Architecture, attention, tokenization, sampling, and the failure modes interviewers actually probe.',
  questions: [
{ q: 'What are foundation models, and how have they changed AI engineering?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `A foundation model is a large model pretrained on broad, mostly unlabeled data with a self-supervised objective, then adapted to many downstream tasks rather than being trained for one. The economic shift is that the expensive step — pretraining — happens once, and adaptation via prompting, retrieval, or lightweight fine-tuning is cheap.<br><br>This inverted the job. Before, an ML engineer collected labels and trained a task-specific model; now the default move is to take a capable general model and spend effort on context, evaluation, and orchestration. Data work shifted from labeling to curation and eval-set design.<br><br><strong>The insight:</strong> foundation models turned most AI work into a systems and evaluation problem rather than a modeling problem. The hard parts are now retrieval quality, prompt and context management, latency/cost budgets, and measuring whether the thing actually works — not choosing an architecture.` },

{ q: 'What is a Large Language Model (LLM), and how does it work?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `An LLM is a neural network — almost always a decoder-only Transformer — trained to predict the next token given all previous tokens. Training minimizes cross-entropy over trillions of tokens; the model learns a conditional distribution P(next token | context).<br><br>At inference it runs autoregressively: encode the prompt into tokens, forward-pass to get logits over the vocabulary, apply temperature/top-p to sample one token, append it to the context, and repeat. Everything the model "knows" lives in its weights as a compressed statistical model of its training corpus.<br><br><strong>What separates a strong answer:</strong> stating that there is no retrieval, database, or reasoning engine inside — capabilities like arithmetic or code generation are emergent side effects of compressing enough text well. That framing immediately explains hallucination (the model always produces a plausible continuation, never a lookup) and why knowledge cutoffs and RAG exist.` },

{ q: 'Inside ChatGPT: What happens after you hit Enter?', tags: [], a: `Your text is tokenized, wrapped in a chat template (system/user/assistant role markers the model was fine-tuned on), and concatenated with prior conversation turns plus any retrieved or tool context. That token sequence goes to the model.<br><br>Generation splits into two phases. <strong>Prefill</strong> processes the whole prompt in parallel in one forward pass, building the KV cache — compute-bound, and it determines time-to-first-token. <strong>Decode</strong> then emits one token at a time, each step re-reading model weights and the growing KV cache from HBM — memory-bandwidth-bound, and it determines tokens-per-second. Tokens stream back as they are produced.<br><br>Around this sits the serving system: a request queue, continuous batching that folds your sequence in with others, safety/moderation classifiers, and stop-sequence handling. <strong>The insight:</strong> the two phases have opposite bottlenecks, which is why TTFT and throughput are optimized with completely different techniques.` },

{ q: 'What is the Transformer architecture and how does it work?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `The Transformer replaced recurrence with attention. Each layer has two sublayers — multi-head self-attention, which lets every position mix information from other positions, and a position-wise feed-forward network — each wrapped in a residual connection and layer normalization.<br><br>The decisive property is that self-attention over a sequence is a set of matrix multiplications with no sequential dependency across positions, so the entire sequence trains in parallel on a GPU. RNNs must process token t before t+1, which caps hardware utilization and makes long-range gradient flow fragile.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff to name:</strong> parallelism is bought with O(n²) attention cost in sequence length and no built-in notion of order (hence positional encodings). Every major follow-up — Flash Attention, sparse/linear attention, RoPE, GQA — is an attempt to keep the parallelism while attacking one of those two costs.` },

{ q: 'What are the key components of the Transformer architecture?', tags: [], a: `Working bottom-up in a decoder-only stack: a <strong>token embedding</strong> table mapping ids to vectors; <strong>positional information</strong> (sinusoidal, learned, or rotary applied inside attention); then N identical blocks.<br><br>Each block contains <strong>multi-head self-attention</strong> (Q/K/V projections, scaled dot-product, causal mask, output projection), a <strong>feed-forward network</strong> (typically up-project 4x, nonlinearity, down-project — where most parameters live), <strong>residual connections</strong> around both sublayers, and <strong>normalization</strong> (modern models use pre-norm RMSNorm for training stability). Finally an output projection to vocabulary logits, often weight-tied to the embedding table.<br><br><strong>Worth stating:</strong> attention is the only component that moves information <em>between</em> positions; the FFN operates on each position independently. That split is the useful mental model — attention routes, FFN computes and stores knowledge.` },

{ q: 'What is tokenization in LLMs?', tags: ['FREQUENTLY ASKED'], a: `Tokenization converts raw text into integer ids from a fixed vocabulary (typically 32k–256k entries). Modern LLMs use subword tokenization — a middle ground between character-level (sequences too long) and word-level (vocabulary unbounded, every typo is an unknown token).<br><br>Subwords keep common words as single tokens while decomposing rare ones into pieces, so any string is representable with no out-of-vocabulary failure. English averages roughly 3–4 characters per token; code, non-Latin scripts, and rare domain terms fragment far worse.<br><br><strong>The practical consequences interviewers look for:</strong> tokenization is why models fail at character-level tasks like counting letters in a word (the model never sees letters), why non-English text costs 2–3x more per unit of meaning, why numbers do arithmetic poorly when split inconsistently, and why your context and cost budgets are measured in tokens rather than words.` },

{ q: 'Explain BPE (Byte Pair Encoding).', tags: [], a: `BPE builds a vocabulary greedily by frequency. Start with the base alphabet (bytes, in byte-level BPE), count all adjacent symbol pairs in the training corpus, merge the most frequent pair into a new symbol, and repeat until the vocabulary reaches target size. The ordered list of merges <em>is</em> the tokenizer; encoding replays those merges on new text.<br><br>Byte-level BPE (GPT-2 onward) starts from the 256 raw bytes, which guarantees any Unicode string encodes without an unknown token — that is why it became the default.<br><br><strong>The subtlety worth raising:</strong> because merges are frequency-driven on the tokenizer's own training corpus, a domain absent from that corpus fragments badly. It also means BPE is deterministic and lossless but not semantically aware — merges can straddle meaningful boundaries, which contributes to arithmetic weakness and inconsistent handling of whitespace and casing.` },

{ q: 'Explain WordPiece and SentencePiece.', tags: [], a: `<strong>WordPiece</strong> (BERT) is BPE's cousin: same merge loop, but instead of picking the most frequent pair it picks the pair maximizing likelihood of the training data — roughly, the pair whose merge most improves a unigram language model score. It marks continuation pieces with <code>##</code>.<br><br><strong>SentencePiece</strong> is not an algorithm but a framework — it can run BPE or Unigram — whose key idea is treating input as a raw Unicode stream including spaces (encoded as ▁) rather than requiring pre-tokenization on whitespace. That makes it language-agnostic and fully reversible, which matters for Chinese, Japanese, and Thai where whitespace does not delimit words. Its <strong>Unigram</strong> mode works subtractively: start with a large candidate vocabulary and prune pieces that cost the least likelihood.<br><br><strong>The practical distinction:</strong> BPE/WordPiece assume you can split on spaces first; SentencePiece does not, which is why multilingual models overwhelmingly use it.` },

{ q: 'What is positional encoding, and why is it needed in Transformers?', tags: ['FREQUENTLY ASKED'], a: `Self-attention is permutation-equivariant — it computes weighted sums over a set, so shuffling input positions shuffles outputs identically. Without positional information, "dog bites man" and "man bites dog" produce the same representations.<br><br>Positional encoding injects order. <strong>Sinusoidal</strong> (original paper) adds fixed sine/cosine waves of varying frequency to embeddings — no parameters, theoretically extrapolates. <strong>Learned absolute</strong> (BERT, GPT-2) trains a position embedding table — simple but hard-capped at trained length. <strong>Rotary (RoPE)</strong> rotates Q and K by a position-dependent angle inside attention, making attention scores depend on relative offset. <strong>ALiBi</strong> adds a distance-proportional penalty to attention logits.<br><br><strong>The trend to name:</strong> the field moved from absolute to relative schemes because what matters for language is how far apart tokens are, not their absolute index — and relative schemes extrapolate to longer contexts far better.` },

{ q: 'What are embeddings?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `An embedding is a learned dense vector representing a discrete item — token, sentence, image, user — where geometric proximity encodes semantic similarity. They exist because raw symbols are not comparable; you cannot subtract "king" from "queen" until both are points in a space where distance means something.<br><br>Distinguish two things that get conflated: <strong>token embeddings</strong> inside an LLM (a lookup table, contextualized by later layers) versus <strong>sentence/document embeddings</strong> from a dedicated model like a bi-encoder, produced by pooling and trained with contrastive objectives for retrieval.<br><br><strong>The insight:</strong> the geometry is entirely a product of the training objective, not a universal property of meaning. Two embedding models of identical dimensionality are not interchangeable, cosine similarity scores are not comparable across models, and you cannot mix vectors from different models in one index — a mistake that silently degrades retrieval rather than throwing an error.` },

{ q: 'Explain the Query (Q), Key (K), and Value (V) in attention.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Each token's representation is projected by three learned matrices into a query, a key, and a value. The retrieval analogy: the <strong>query</strong> is what this token is looking for, the <strong>key</strong> is what each token advertises about itself, and the <strong>value</strong> is the content actually delivered when matched.<br><br>Mechanically: score every query against every key via dot product (high dot product = relevant), scale by √d_k, softmax into weights summing to 1, then output the weighted sum of values. In self-attention Q, K, V all come from the same sequence; in cross-attention Q comes from one sequence and K/V from another.<br><br><strong>Why separate K and V rather than one vector:</strong> it decouples <em>matching</em> from <em>content</em>. A token can be easy to find on one basis while contributing something different once found — a flexibility a single shared vector cannot express. This split is also exactly what the KV cache stores.` },

{ q: 'What is self-attention, and how does it work in Transformers?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Self-attention lets every position build its new representation as a weighted blend of all positions in the same sequence, with weights computed from content rather than fixed by position. Formally: <code>Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QKᵀ/√d_k)V</code>, with Q, K, V all derived from the same input.<br><br>The consequence is that any two tokens are one hop apart regardless of distance — a path length of O(1) versus O(n) in an RNN — so long-range dependencies do not have to survive many sequential steps and vanishing gradients.<br><br><strong>The cost:</strong> the QKᵀ matrix is n×n, so both compute and memory scale quadratically with sequence length. At 128k context that matrix is the dominant cost, which is the entire motivation for Flash Attention (never materialize it), sparse/sliding-window attention (compute only part of it), and linear attention variants (approximate it).` },

{ q: 'What is cross-attention in Transformers?', tags: [], a: `Cross-attention is attention where queries come from one sequence and keys/values from another. In an encoder-decoder model the decoder's queries attend over the encoder's outputs, which is how translation conditions each generated token on the source sentence.<br><br>The mechanics are identical to self-attention — same scaled dot product, same softmax — only the source of K and V differs. It is typically not causally masked, since the whole conditioning sequence is available.<br><br><strong>Where it matters today:</strong> encoder-decoder models (T5, Whisper), diffusion models conditioning image generation on text prompts, and many multimodal architectures where a vision encoder's patch embeddings are cross-attended by a language decoder. In decoder-only LLMs there is no cross-attention — external information is simply concatenated into the prompt, which is why RAG works by string-stuffing rather than by an architectural mechanism.` },

{ q: 'Why do we scale dot-product attention by √dₖ?', tags: ['FREQUENTLY ASKED'], a: `Assume query and key components are roughly independent with mean 0 and variance 1. Their dot product over d_k dimensions then has variance d_k, so its magnitude grows like √d_k. At d_k = 128 the raw scores swing across a range wide enough to push softmax into saturation.<br><br>A saturated softmax is nearly one-hot: one weight ≈ 1, the rest ≈ 0. The gradient of softmax is proportional to p(1−p), so it vanishes at both extremes — training stalls in exactly the layers you need to learn routing.<br><br>Dividing by √d_k normalizes variance back to ~1 regardless of head dimension, keeping logits in a range where softmax stays soft and gradients flow. <strong>The check that proves you understand it:</strong> it is √d_k, not √d_model — the scaling depends on per-head dimension, which is why multi-head attention with smaller heads stays numerically well-behaved.` },

{ q: 'What is causal masking?', tags: [], a: `Causal (autoregressive) masking prevents position i from attending to any position j > i. It is implemented by adding −∞ to the upper-triangular entries of the attention score matrix before softmax, which drives those weights to exactly zero.<br><br>It exists so that a single forward pass over a sequence yields a valid training signal at every position simultaneously — position i predicts token i+1 using only tokens ≤ i, so one pass gives n training examples without the model cheating by seeing the answer. Without the mask the objective is trivially solvable by copying.<br><br><strong>The connection worth making:</strong> causal masking is precisely what makes the KV cache valid at inference. Because past tokens can never attend to future ones, their K and V vectors are final once computed and can be cached and reused — a property bidirectional models like BERT do not have.` },

{ q: 'What is multi-head attention, and why use multiple heads?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Instead of one attention operation over the full d_model dimensions, split into h heads each of dimension d_model/h, run attention independently in each, concatenate, and project. Total compute is roughly unchanged — you have partitioned the dimensions, not multiplied the work.<br><br>The motivation is representational. A single softmax attention produces one weighted average per position, so it can essentially attend to one thing; averaging over multiple relationships blurs them together. Separate heads can specialize — empirically some track syntactic dependencies, some do positional/previous-token copying, some handle coreference — and the output projection recombines them.<br><br><strong>The modern tradeoff:</strong> at inference each head needs its own cached K and V, so KV cache size scales with head count. That memory pressure is exactly what MQA and GQA attack by sharing K/V across query heads while keeping query heads distinct.` },

{ q: 'What are feed-forward networks in LLMs?', tags: [], a: `The FFN is a position-wise MLP applied identically and independently to every token: up-project from d_model to roughly 4×d_model, apply a nonlinearity (GELU, or SwiGLU in modern models), project back down. It contains about two-thirds of a Transformer's parameters.<br><br>It is where per-token computation and, on current evidence, most factual knowledge lives — the up-projection/down-projection pair behaves like a key-value memory, where up-projection neurons act as pattern detectors and the down-projection writes associated content back into the residual stream.<br><br><strong>The clean division of labor to state:</strong> attention moves information between positions, the FFN transforms information within a position. That framing explains why MoE targets the FFN specifically — it is the parameter-heavy component, so routing each token to only a couple of expert FFNs cuts compute dramatically while keeping attention (the mixing mechanism) intact.` },

{ q: 'What is the context window, and why does it matter?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `The context window is the maximum number of tokens the model can attend over in one forward pass — prompt, retrieved documents, conversation history, tool outputs, and the generation itself all share that budget.<br><br>It matters because it is a hard architectural limit, not a soft preference. Exceeding it forces truncation or summarization, and both lose information silently. It bounds how much of a document you can process at once, how long a conversation stays coherent, and how many few-shot examples or retrieved chunks fit.<br><br><strong>The nuance that separates a strong answer:</strong> usable context is shorter than advertised context. Attention degrades over distance, models exhibit the "lost in the middle" effect where information in the center of a long prompt is retrieved worse than at the ends, and cost/latency scale with tokens. A 200k window does not mean you should fill it — targeted retrieval of 4k relevant tokens typically beats dumping 100k mediocre ones.` },

{ q: 'Why is the context window limited?', tags: [], a: `Three compounding constraints. <strong>Compute:</strong> self-attention is O(n²) in sequence length, so 4x the context is 16x the attention cost. <strong>Memory:</strong> the KV cache grows linearly with length × batch × layers × heads, and at long context it dominates GPU memory — often exceeding the weights themselves. <strong>Training distribution:</strong> the model only learns to use positions it saw during training, and long-document training data is scarce and expensive.<br><br>Positional encoding is a fourth limit — learned absolute embeddings simply have no entry beyond trained length, though RoPE with interpolation/scaling extends this.<br><br><strong>What actually moved the frontier:</strong> Flash Attention removed the memory wall by never materializing the n×n matrix (compute is still quadratic, but IO is linear), GQA/MQA shrank the KV cache, RoPE scaling extended positions, and staged long-context fine-tuning taught models to use the extra room. Long context is an engineering achievement, not a free parameter.` },

{ q: 'What is temperature, and how does it affect output?', tags: ['FREQUENTLY ASKED'], a: `Temperature T divides the logits before softmax: <code>softmax(logits / T)</code>. T &lt; 1 sharpens the distribution toward high-probability tokens; T &gt; 1 flattens it, raising the chance of unlikely tokens; T → 0 approaches greedy argmax decoding.<br><br>Practically: T ≈ 0 for extraction, classification, structured output, and anything needing reproducibility; T ≈ 0.7–1.0 for creative writing and brainstorming where diversity is the point.<br><br><strong>Two things worth correcting:</strong> first, temperature does not make a model more or less "accurate" — it reshapes a distribution the model already has, so low temperature makes it confidently repeat its most likely answer, including a confidently wrong one. Second, T=0 is not fully deterministic in practice on GPU serving stacks, because batching and floating-point non-associativity can reorder reductions and flip near-ties. Do not promise bit-exact reproducibility on that basis alone.` },

{ q: 'Why is the first token slower than the rest?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Because generation has two distinct phases with different cost profiles. The first token requires <strong>prefill</strong>: a full forward pass over the entire prompt to build the KV cache. That is O(n²) attention over n prompt tokens and is compute-bound, saturating the GPU's matrix units.<br><br>Every subsequent token is a <strong>decode</strong> step processing exactly one new token against the cached keys and values — a small amount of arithmetic dominated by the time to stream model weights and the KV cache out of HBM. It is memory-bandwidth-bound.<br><br>So time-to-first-token scales with prompt length while inter-token latency stays roughly flat. <strong>The engineering consequence:</strong> the two phases need different optimizations — TTFT improves with prompt caching, chunked prefill, and shorter prompts; throughput improves with continuous batching, GQA, quantized KV cache, and speculative decoding. Serving systems increasingly disaggregate prefill and decode onto separate hardware pools for exactly this reason.` },

{ q: 'Explain top-p (nucleus) and top-k sampling. How do they differ?', tags: [], a: `Both truncate the distribution before sampling to avoid drawing from the long tail of garbage tokens. <strong>Top-k</strong> keeps the k highest-probability tokens and renormalizes — a fixed count. <strong>Top-p</strong> keeps the smallest set whose cumulative probability ≥ p — a fixed probability mass, so the count adapts.<br><br>That adaptivity is the whole argument for top-p. When the model is confident (one token at 0.95), top-p=0.9 keeps essentially one candidate while top-k=50 admits 49 bad ones. When the model is genuinely uncertain across many plausible continuations, top-p widens automatically while top-k stays artificially narrow.<br><br><strong>Practical guidance:</strong> top-p is the better default; use one or the other rather than stacking both with temperature and getting an unpredictable interaction. For structured or factual output, constrain harder (low temperature, or grammar-constrained decoding) rather than tuning sampling — sampling parameters manage the diversity/coherence tradeoff, they do not manage correctness.` },

{ q: 'What are logits, and how are they used in text generation?', tags: [], a: `Logits are the raw, unnormalized scores the final linear layer produces over the entire vocabulary — one real number per token, typically 32k–256k of them per step. They live in (−∞, ∞) and only become probabilities after softmax.<br><br>The generation pipeline operates on logits in order: apply repetition/frequency penalties, apply logit bias or masking (banning tokens, or constraining to a grammar/JSON schema), divide by temperature, softmax, truncate with top-k/top-p, then sample.<br><br><strong>Why the distinction matters in practice:</strong> logits are where you get real control. Constrained decoding works by masking every token that would violate a schema to −∞, which makes invalid output structurally impossible rather than merely discouraged by prompting. Logits also give you log-probabilities for confidence estimation, perplexity, and self-consistency scoring — though note that raw token probability is a poor calibrated confidence signal for factuality on RLHF-tuned models.` },

{ q: 'What are skip (residual) connections in Transformers?', tags: [], a: `A residual connection adds a sublayer's input to its output: <code>x + Sublayer(x)</code>. Every attention and FFN block in a Transformer is wrapped this way.<br><br>They solve trainability at depth. The addition creates a direct gradient path — the derivative of the identity branch is 1 — so gradients reach early layers without being repeatedly multiplied by small Jacobians. Without residuals, 32+ layer Transformers do not train at all.<br><br><strong>The framing that shows depth of understanding:</strong> residuals turn the network into a "residual stream" — a shared communication channel that each block reads from and writes an increment into, rather than a pipeline that fully rewrites the representation at each stage. That perspective explains why layers can be somewhat modular, why pre-norm (normalizing inside the branch, leaving the stream clean) trains more stably than the original post-norm at scale, and it underpins most modern interpretability work.` },

{ q: 'What is the difference between open-source and closed-source LLMs? When would you choose each?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Closed (API):</strong> best frontier capability, zero infrastructure, instant scaling, but data leaves your boundary, per-token pricing that scales with usage, model versions can change or be deprecated under you, and limited control over latency or fine-tuning.<br><br><strong>Open-weights (self-hosted):</strong> full data control, fixed infrastructure cost that amortizes at volume, version pinning, freedom to fine-tune/quantize/distill, and no vendor lock-in — at the cost of GPU capacity planning, serving expertise, and a capability gap on the hardest reasoning tasks.<br><br><strong>How I'd decide:</strong> start on an API to find product-market fit — self-hosting before you know your workload is premature optimization. Move to open weights when one of these fires: regulatory/data-residency requirements, volume where GPU amortization beats per-token pricing, latency needs an API cannot meet, or a narrow task where a fine-tuned small model matches a frontier model at a fraction of cost. Note "open-source" is usually a misnomer — most released weights carry usage restrictions and no training data, so check the actual license.` },

{ q: 'What is the difference between encoder-only, decoder-only, and encoder-decoder architectures?', tags: ['FREQUENTLY ASKED'], a: `<strong>Encoder-only</strong> (BERT): bidirectional attention, every token sees full context both directions, trained with masked language modeling. Excellent representations for classification, NER, and retrieval embeddings — but cannot generate autoregressively.<br><br><strong>Decoder-only</strong> (GPT, Llama, Claude): causal attention, trained on next-token prediction. Generates naturally, supports KV caching, and scales cleanly — which is why it won for general-purpose LLMs.<br><br><strong>Encoder-decoder</strong> (T5, BART, Whisper): bidirectional encoder over the input plus causal decoder cross-attending to it. Natural fit for transduction tasks with a clear input→output boundary — translation, summarization, speech-to-text.<br><br><strong>Why decoder-only dominates:</strong> it is the most general — any task can be cast as text continuation, so one model handles everything with no architectural change, and the single objective scales with data and compute more simply. Encoder-only models are still the right tool for embeddings and rerankers, where you want a representation rather than a continuation.` },

{ q: 'What is the KV cache, and how does it speed up inference?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `During decoding, each new token attends over all previous tokens. Because causal masking means past tokens never depend on future ones, their key and value vectors never change once computed — so you cache them instead of recomputing.<br><br>Without a cache, generating token n requires re-running attention over the whole prefix: O(n²) work per step, O(n³) for the sequence. With it, each step computes K/V for one new token and attends against the cache: O(n) per step. This is not a micro-optimization — it is the difference between usable and unusable generation.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff that makes this a great interview question:</strong> you trade compute for memory, and the memory is large — <code>2 × layers × heads × head_dim × seq_len × batch × dtype_bytes</code>, easily tens of GB at long context and reaching the size of the weights. That cost drives GQA/MQA (fewer K/V heads), paged attention (eliminate fragmentation), quantized KV cache (fp8/int8), and sliding-window or eviction policies.` },

{ q: 'What is model distillation, and how is it used with LLMs?', tags: [], a: `Distillation trains a small student model to imitate a large teacher. Classic knowledge distillation matches the teacher's full output distribution (soft targets) rather than hard labels — the relative probabilities across wrong answers carry information about how the teacher generalizes, often called dark knowledge.<br><br>For LLMs the common practical form is <strong>sequence-level distillation</strong>: generate a large corpus of teacher outputs (often with reasoning traces) and supervise-fine-tune the student on them. This is how most small instruction-tuned models are built.<br><br><strong>Where it breaks, and the honest caveat:</strong> distillation transfers the teacher's <em>behavior on the covered distribution</em>, not its capability. Students reliably match teachers on tasks well represented in the distillation set and fall off sharply on hard multi-step reasoning and out-of-distribution inputs — imitating reasoning traces is not the same as acquiring the capability. Also check the teacher's terms of service; many API providers explicitly prohibit training competing models on their outputs.` },

{ q: 'What is Mixture of Experts (MoE), and how does it work?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `MoE replaces each dense FFN with N expert FFNs plus a small router network. For each token, the router scores experts and activates only the top-k (commonly 2). Attention layers stay dense and shared.<br><br>This decouples parameter count from compute per token. Mixtral 8x7B holds ~47B total parameters but activates ~13B per token, so it has the knowledge capacity of a large model at roughly the inference FLOPs of a much smaller one.<br><br><strong>The costs a strong answer names:</strong> (1) all experts must be resident in VRAM even though most are idle, so memory footprint tracks total parameters, not active ones — MoE saves compute, not memory. (2) Routing can collapse onto a few popular experts, so training needs an auxiliary load-balancing loss. (3) In distributed serving, tokens in a batch route to different experts on different devices, producing all-to-all communication and uneven load that makes throughput harder to predict than a dense model of equal quality.` },

{ q: 'What is the difference between dense and sparse models?', tags: [], a: `A <strong>dense</strong> model activates every parameter for every input — total parameters and per-token compute are the same number. A <strong>sparse</strong> model activates a subset per input; MoE is the dominant form, where routing selects a few expert FFNs per token.<br><br>The distinction matters because it breaks the usual assumption that a bigger model is proportionally more expensive to run. Sparsity buys capacity cheaply in FLOPs.<br><br><strong>Do not conflate this with two other uses of "sparse":</strong> weight sparsity/pruning (zeroing individual weights to shrink the model, which needs hardware support like 2:4 structured sparsity to actually speed anything up) and sparse attention (restricting which positions attend to which, e.g. sliding-window). All three are called sparsity and solve different problems — conditional compute, model size, and quadratic attention cost respectively. In retrieval, "sparse vs dense" means something else again: lexical BM25 vectors versus learned embeddings.` },

{ q: 'What is Flash Attention?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Flash Attention is an IO-aware exact attention algorithm. Standard attention materializes the n×n score matrix in HBM, writing and reading O(n²) data — on modern GPUs that memory traffic, not the arithmetic, is the bottleneck.<br><br>Flash Attention tiles Q, K, V into blocks that fit in on-chip SRAM, computes attention block by block, and uses the online-softmax trick to maintain running max and sum so the final result is numerically identical without ever storing the full matrix. Memory drops from O(n²) to O(n), and wall-clock speeds up 2–4x. For the backward pass it recomputes attention from the stored statistics rather than saving activations — trading extra FLOPs for far less memory traffic, which is a net win.<br><br><strong>The point to emphasize:</strong> it is an <em>exact</em> algorithm, not an approximation like sparse or linear attention — same output, better hardware utilization. That is why it became a default rather than a tradeoff, and why it, more than anything, unlocked long context.` },

{ q: 'What is cross-entropy loss?', tags: [], a: `Cross-entropy measures the cost of encoding the true distribution using the model's predicted distribution: <code>H(p,q) = −Σ p(x) log q(x)</code>. For language modeling the target is one-hot, so it collapses to <code>−log q(correct token)</code> — the negative log-probability the model assigned to the token that actually occurred.<br><br>It is the right loss because minimizing it is equivalent to maximum likelihood estimation, and its gradient with respect to the logits is simply <code>(predicted − target)</code>, which is clean and well-scaled. Squared error on probabilities, by contrast, produces vanishing gradients exactly when the model is confidently wrong.<br><br><strong>The connection worth making:</strong> perplexity is just <code>exp(cross-entropy)</code>, interpretable as the effective number of tokens the model is choosing between. A loss of 2.0 nats means perplexity ~7.4. And because loss is averaged per token, it is tokenizer-dependent — perplexity numbers are not comparable across models with different tokenizers.` },

{ q: 'What is Grouped-Query Attention (GQA), and how does it differ from MHA?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `In standard multi-head attention every query head has its own key and value heads. <strong>Multi-Query Attention (MQA)</strong> takes the extreme opposite — all query heads share a single K/V head — cutting KV cache size by the head count but measurably hurting quality. <strong>GQA</strong> interpolates: partition query heads into G groups, one K/V head per group.<br><br>With 32 query heads and 8 KV groups you get a 4x smaller KV cache at near-MHA quality. That is why Llama 2 70B, Llama 3, and Mistral all use GQA.<br><br><strong>Why this matters more than it sounds:</strong> decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, and the KV cache is a large share of the bytes read per step. Shrinking it 4x directly raises tokens/second and lets you hold more concurrent sequences in memory, which raises batch size and therefore throughput again. GQA is one of the highest-leverage architectural choices for serving cost. Note it must be chosen at pretraining time, though existing MHA checkpoints can be uptrained into GQA by mean-pooling K/V heads.` },

{ q: 'How does Rotary Position Embedding (RoPE) work, and why is it preferred?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `RoPE encodes position by <em>rotating</em> query and key vectors rather than adding anything to embeddings. Treat each pair of dimensions as a 2D plane and rotate it by an angle proportional to the token's position, with a different frequency per pair.<br><br>The elegance: the dot product of a query at position m with a key at position n depends only on (m − n). Absolute rotations produce relative attention scores for free, with no extra parameters and no change to the attention computation itself.<br><br><strong>Why it beat learned absolute embeddings:</strong> (1) relative structure matches how language works — what matters is distance, not index; (2) it applies inside attention to Q and K only, leaving the residual stream clean; (3) it extends to longer contexts via frequency interpolation or NTK-aware scaling (adjusting the base θ), so you can stretch a 4k model toward 128k with modest continued fine-tuning. Learned tables simply have no embedding beyond trained length. RoPE is not free extrapolation — naive extension degrades — but it is <em>adaptable</em>, which absolute embeddings are not.` },

{ q: 'Explain Layer Normalization.', tags: [], a: `LayerNorm normalizes each token's activation vector across its own feature dimension: subtract the mean, divide by the standard deviation, then apply learned scale (γ) and shift (β). Crucially it operates per-sample, so unlike BatchNorm it has no dependence on batch statistics — which is why it works with variable-length sequences, small batches, and autoregressive inference where batch composition changes constantly.<br><br>It stabilizes training by keeping activation scale consistent across layers, preventing the drift that makes deep stacks diverge.<br><br><strong>The placement detail that matters:</strong> the original Transformer used <strong>post-norm</strong> (normalize after the residual add), which requires careful learning-rate warmup and becomes unstable past a few dozen layers. Modern LLMs use <strong>pre-norm</strong> (normalize the input to each sublayer, leaving the residual path unnormalized), which keeps a clean identity path for gradients and trains reliably at depth. Post-norm arguably has slightly better final quality when it converges — pre-norm wins because it converges.` },

{ q: 'Explain RMSNorm.', tags: [], a: `RMSNorm drops the mean-centering step from LayerNorm. It divides by the root-mean-square of the activations and applies a learned scale, with no mean subtraction and typically no bias: <code>x / sqrt(mean(x²) + ε) × γ</code>.<br><br>The empirical finding motivating it is that re-centering contributes little to LayerNorm's benefit — the re-scaling does the work. Removing the mean saves one pass over the vector and a reduction, giving roughly 7–15% faster normalization and fewer memory reads.<br><br><strong>Why this small change is worth knowing:</strong> normalization runs twice per layer across 80+ layers, so a modest per-call saving compounds into real end-to-end throughput. Llama, Mistral, Gemma, and most recent open models use RMSNorm with pre-norm placement — it is the current default. It is a good example of the pattern where architectural progress in LLMs often comes from removing components that turn out not to be load-bearing (also see: no bias terms in linear layers).` },

{ q: 'Your LLM keeps ignoring instructions. How do you enforce structured output?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Escalate through mechanisms of increasing strength rather than rewriting the prompt repeatedly.<br><br><strong>1. Constrained decoding</strong> — the only approach with a hard guarantee. Mask logits at each step so only tokens valid under your JSON schema or grammar can be sampled (outlines, llguidance, vLLM's structured output, or the provider's native structured-output/JSON-schema mode). Invalid output becomes structurally impossible.<br><br><strong>2. Native tool/function calling</strong> — models are fine-tuned specifically for this, so adherence is far higher than free-form prompting.<br><br><strong>3. Prompt-level fixes</strong> if neither is available: put the schema last (recency helps), prefill the assistant turn with the opening <code>{</code>, set temperature to 0, and give one or two exact-format examples.<br><br><strong>4. Always parse-and-repair</strong> at the boundary — validate with Pydantic/zod, and on failure retry once with the parse error fed back.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff to state:</strong> heavy constraint can degrade content quality, because forcing a format the model finds unnatural competes with the reasoning. If quality drops, let the model reason freely first and emit structure in a second, cheap, constrained call.` },

{ q: 'Your tool hits the context limit on long documents. How do you handle it?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `First diagnose whether the task is <em>selective</em> (the answer lives in a few passages) or <em>holistic</em> (it requires the whole document). They have different solutions and conflating them is the common mistake.<br><br><strong>Selective — use retrieval.</strong> Chunk, embed, retrieve top-k relevant passages, and answer over those. Cheaper and usually more accurate than stuffing the full document, since irrelevant context actively degrades attention.<br><br><strong>Holistic — use hierarchical processing.</strong> Map-reduce (summarize chunks independently, then summarize the summaries) parallelizes well; refine (carry a running summary across chunks sequentially) preserves narrative order but cannot parallelize and can drift.<br><br><strong>Supporting moves:</strong> chunk on semantic boundaries with overlap so you never split mid-argument; compress context (drop boilerplate, extract only relevant sentences); for conversations, keep a rolling summary plus the last N turns verbatim.<br><br><strong>The thing people miss:</strong> a bigger context window is often the wrong fix. Cost and latency scale with tokens, and "lost in the middle" means a 100k-token prompt can retrieve worse than a well-targeted 4k one. Measure before buying context.` },

{ q: 'Your LLM will not admit uncertainty. How do you make it say "I don\'t know"?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `The root cause is training, not prompting: next-token prediction and RLHF both reward fluent, confident, helpful-sounding completions, and human raters rarely prefer "I don't know." Abstention is systematically trained out.<br><br><strong>Grounding is the highest-leverage fix.</strong> Give the model a closed context and instruct it to answer only from that context, replying with an explicit refusal token when the answer is absent. Verifying "is this in the provided text" is a far easier task than introspecting on parametric knowledge. Include few-shot examples that <em>demonstrate</em> abstention — without them the model has no in-context evidence that refusing is acceptable.<br><br><strong>Then add a verification layer:</strong> a separate grounding/NLI check on whether each claim is entailed by the retrieved context, self-consistency (sample n times — high disagreement signals uncertainty far better than the model's stated confidence), and a reranker score threshold below which you refuse before generating at all.<br><br><strong>The honest caveat:</strong> verbalized confidence from an RLHF'd model is poorly calibrated — asking "how sure are you, 0–100?" produces confident-sounding numbers that do not track accuracy. Trust sampling-based or external checks over self-report, and expect a precision/recall tradeoff: tuning for more abstention will refuse some answerable questions.` },

{ q: 'Your LLM is too verbose. How do you control response length?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Instruction-level:</strong> specify a concrete budget ("answer in under 50 words", "exactly 3 bullets") rather than vague terms like "be brief" — models comply with countable constraints far better, though token counting is approximate since the model cannot count its own tokens reliably. Give a few-shot example at the target length; demonstration beats description.<br><br><strong>Format-level:</strong> constrain output structure. A JSON schema with fixed fields, or <code>max_tokens</code> as a hard stop — but note <code>max_tokens</code> truncates mid-sentence rather than shortening gracefully, so use it as a safety rail, not a length control.<br><br><strong>Prompt hygiene:</strong> remove instructions that implicitly reward length ("explain thoroughly", "be comprehensive"), and drop chain-of-thought for tasks that do not need it — or move reasoning into a separate call whose output you discard.<br><br><strong>The durable fix</strong> if this is a persistent product requirement: fine-tune on your target length distribution. Verbosity is an RLHF artifact (raters prefer longer answers), so you are fighting the base model's prior — a few hundred correctly-sized examples resets it more reliably than any prompt.` },

{ q: 'Your LLM leaks memorized training data. How do you prevent this?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Memorization is a real property of LLMs — models provably reproduce verbatim sequences from training data, and the risk rises with duplicate documents, high-entropy strings like keys and credentials, and larger models.<br><br><strong>Prevent at the data layer (most effective):</strong> deduplicate the corpus aggressively — duplication is the single strongest predictor of memorization. Scrub PII and secrets before training with automated detectors, and exclude sensitive corpora entirely.<br><br><strong>Prevent at training time:</strong> differential privacy (DP-SGD) gives formal guarantees but costs utility and compute; often the better tradeoff is simply not putting the data in pretraining and using retrieval instead — with RAG, sensitive data lives in an access-controlled store, subject to per-user permissions, and can be deleted for real.<br><br><strong>Mitigate at inference:</strong> output filters scanning for PII patterns and n-gram overlap against known sensitive documents; block long verbatim matches.<br><br><strong>The point worth making:</strong> you cannot reliably delete a fact from trained weights — machine unlearning is an open research problem, not a production tool. So the architectural decision is: anything that might need deletion, revocation, or per-user access control belongs in a retrieval store, never in training data. That is a compliance argument for RAG, not just a freshness one.` },

{ q: 'Your coding assistant generates outdated, deprecated code. How do you fix it?', tags: [], a: `The cause is the knowledge cutoff plus training-data skew: the corpus contains years of accumulated old code, so the statistically likely completion is the historically common API, not the current one.<br><br><strong>Ground it in current documentation.</strong> Retrieve the actual API reference for the libraries in play and put it in context — this directly counteracts the stale prior and is the highest-value fix. Feed in the project's real dependency versions from <code>package.json</code>/<code>requirements.txt</code> so the model targets the version you actually run.<br><br><strong>Give it the repo's own context.</strong> Retrieved examples of how your codebase already calls the library are stronger evidence than any instruction — the model imitates in-context patterns over parametric habits.<br><br><strong>Close the loop with verification.</strong> The durable fix is not making the model right, it is catching when it is wrong: run generated code against the real toolchain, surface deprecation warnings and type errors, and feed failures back for a repair pass. Linters and CI are far more reliable than prompting.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> pin the doc snapshot you retrieve from. If you retrieve from live docs and the library ships a breaking change, your assistant's behavior changes with no deploy on your side — that is an untracked dependency.` },

{ q: 'Your tokenizer splits domain terms into meaningless pieces. How do you fix it?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Confirm the problem first — inspect the actual token ids for your key terms. Fragmentation matters when it inflates cost, wastes context, and forces the model to reassemble meaning across pieces, which measurably hurts on domain-specific tasks (medical codes, chemical names, part numbers, non-Latin scripts).<br><br><strong>Options, in order of cost:</strong><br>(1) <em>Prompt-level workaround</em> — define terms once in context and let attention bind the pieces. Free, and often enough.<br>(2) <em>Add tokens to the vocabulary</em> and resize the embedding matrix. The trap: new embeddings are randomly initialized and mean nothing, so this <em>requires</em> continued pretraining on domain text to be useful. Initializing new embeddings as the mean of their constituent subword embeddings is a good warm start.<br>(3) <em>Train a domain tokenizer from scratch</em> — best compression, but it invalidates the pretrained model entirely, so it only makes sense if you are pretraining anyway.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff to state plainly:</strong> vocabulary changes break checkpoint compatibility and every cached embedding, and a larger vocabulary grows the embedding and output layers. For most applications the fragmentation cost is smaller than the cost of retraining — measure the actual task-level degradation before touching the tokenizer.` },

{ q: 'Your KV cache grows too large during long generation. How do you manage memory?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Size it first: <code>2 × layers × kv_heads × head_dim × seq_len × batch × bytes</code>. Knowing which term dominates tells you which lever to pull.<br><br><strong>Architectural (requires the right model):</strong> GQA/MQA cuts the kv_heads term 4–8x — the single biggest win, which is why modern models ship with it.<br><br><strong>Serving-level (highest practical leverage):</strong> <em>PagedAttention</em> (vLLM) stores the cache in fixed-size non-contiguous blocks like OS virtual memory, eliminating the internal fragmentation that otherwise wastes 60–80% of allocated cache, and enabling copy-on-write sharing of common prefixes across requests. <em>Quantize the cache</em> to fp8/int8 for a 2–4x cut at minimal quality loss — cheaper than quantizing weights and usually safer.<br><br><strong>Algorithmic (lossy — measure before shipping):</strong> sliding-window attention keeps only the last W tokens; StreamingLLM keeps the first few "attention sink" tokens plus a recent window, which preserves fluency far better than a naive window; H2O and similar policies evict low-attention tokens.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> eviction is silent information loss. The model will not tell you it lost the middle of the document — it will just answer worse. Validate any eviction policy against a long-context retrieval eval, not just perplexity.` },

{ q: 'Your Transformer OOMs on long documents from quadratic attention. How do you scale it?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Separate the two costs, because they have different fixes. Attention <em>memory</em> is O(n²) only in naive implementations; attention <em>compute</em> is genuinely O(n²).<br><br><strong>Fix memory first — it is free.</strong> Flash Attention makes memory O(n) by tiling and never materializing the score matrix, with identical numerics. If you are OOMing on long sequences and not using it, that is the answer before anything else. Add gradient checkpointing for training (recompute activations instead of storing them, ~30% slower, large memory saving) and mixed precision.<br><br><strong>Then attack compute, accepting approximation:</strong> sliding-window attention (Mistral) makes cost linear with a receptive field that still grows across layers; sparse patterns (Longformer's local+global, BigBird) keep a few global tokens; linear attention variants and state-space models (Mamba) drop to O(n) but historically trade off exact recall.<br><br><strong>Or avoid long context entirely:</strong> retrieval over chunks is often both cheaper and more accurate than processing a whole document.<br><br><strong>The honest framing:</strong> exact long attention is expensive, and every sub-quadratic method trades some ability to attend precisely to arbitrary distant tokens. Choose based on whether your task needs sharp long-range recall (keep exact attention + retrieval) or diffuse long-range context (approximations are fine).` },

{ q: 'Your distilled student fails on complex reasoning the teacher handled. How do you close the gap?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Diagnose the gap type first. If the student fails on problems <em>similar</em> to the distillation set, it is a data problem. If it fails only on problems requiring more sequential steps, it may be a capacity problem — and those need different responses.<br><br><strong>Data-side fixes:</strong> distill on reasoning traces, not just final answers — chain-of-thought distillation transfers the intermediate steps and substantially outperforms answer-only supervision. Target the distillation set at the failure distribution rather than the average case; hard examples are where the teacher's advantage lives, and uniformly sampled data underrepresents them. Sample multiple teacher solutions per problem and keep the correct ones (rejection sampling), which both filters teacher errors and adds diversity.<br><br><strong>Training-side:</strong> match intermediate representations or attention patterns, not only output logits; consider a teaching-assistant model when the capacity gap is very large, since a 400x jump distills worse than two 20x jumps.<br><br><strong>Inference-side:</strong> let the student spend more compute — self-consistency over several samples, or a verifier that reranks candidates.<br><br><strong>The uncomfortable part:</strong> below some scale the gap does not close. Multi-step reasoning appears to need capacity, and imitating traces is not the same as acquiring the capability — students learn the <em>form</em> of reasoning and still fail when the problem requires genuinely composing steps. If evals say the student cannot get there, route hard queries to the teacher rather than pretending distillation solved it.` },

{ q: 'After RLHF your model got safer but weaker. How do you manage the alignment tax?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Confirm it is real and locate it — measure capability benchmarks before and after alignment, split by category. Often the "capability loss" is actually over-refusal on benign prompts, which is a very different fix than genuine degradation.<br><br><strong>Training-side mitigations:</strong> KL regularization against the reference model is the standard control — it penalizes drifting too far from the pretrained policy, and the KL coefficient is the direct alignment-tax dial. Mix pretraining data into the RLHF stage (PPO-ptx) to prevent catastrophic forgetting. Balance the preference data: if it is dominated by safety comparisons, the reward model learns that refusing is generically good, so include helpfulness and hard-task comparisons in the same mix.<br><br><strong>Architectural alternatives:</strong> keep alignment in a separate lightweight layer — LoRA adapters you can swap or scale down, or moving policy enforcement to input/output classifiers so the base model stays capable and the guardrail is a separate, tunable component. That also makes safety auditable and updatable without retraining.<br><br><strong>The framing that matters:</strong> the alignment tax is a real tradeoff curve, not a bug to eliminate. The engineering question is where on the curve you sit and whether you can see it — which requires evals that measure over-refusal (false-refusal rate on benign prompts) alongside safety and capability. Teams that only measure safety will happily ship a model that refuses everything.` },

{ q: 'Your RLHF model is gaming the reward model. How do you fix reward hacking?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Reward hacking is Goodhart's law in its purest form: the reward model is a learned <em>proxy</em> for human preference, and optimizing hard against any proxy eventually finds inputs where the proxy and the true objective diverge. Classic symptoms are length inflation, sycophancy, confident formatting over substance, and reward-model-specific artifacts.<br><br><strong>Detect it:</strong> track reward score against held-out human evaluation. The signature is reward climbing while human ratings plateau or fall — if you only watch reward, you will not see it happen.<br><br><strong>Constrain optimization:</strong> KL penalty against the reference policy is the primary lever (hacking usually requires drifting far off-distribution), plus early stopping — more optimization is not better once the curves diverge.<br><br><strong>Improve the proxy:</strong> retrain the reward model on the policy's <em>current</em> outputs, since hacking exploits regions where the RM was never trained (iterated/online RLHF). Ensemble reward models and take the minimum or penalize disagreement, since different RMs are hacked in different places. Explicitly debias known artifacts like length.<br><br><strong>Or change the setup:</strong> DPO removes the separate reward model, eliminating that specific failure surface (though the preference data's own biases remain). For verifiable domains — math, code — use ground-truth execution rewards, which cannot be hacked in the same way.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> once a hack is in the policy it becomes training data for the next RM iteration, so the loop can entrench it. Human spot-checks on a fixed eval set are the circuit breaker.` },

{ q: 'Your chatbot loses context after 10 turns. How do you maintain long conversations?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Diagnose which of three things is happening — they look identical to a user and have different fixes. (1) You are truncating history to fit the window. (2) History fits but the model attends poorly across a long prompt ("lost in the middle"). (3) Important facts appeared early and have been pushed out or diluted.<br><br><strong>The standard architecture is hybrid memory:</strong> keep the last N turns verbatim (recency is what conversational coherence needs), maintain a rolling summary of older turns, and extract durable facts — user preferences, constraints, decisions — into a separate structured store that is always injected regardless of recency. Retrieve semantically from older turns only when relevant to the current message.<br><br>The structured-fact store is the part people skip and the part that matters most: "user is vegetarian" must survive 200 turns, and summarization erodes exactly these details as it recursively compresses.<br><br><strong>Second-order failure:</strong> recursive summarization compounds error — each pass is a lossy re-encoding of a lossy encoding, so facts drift and then become confidently wrong. Summarize from the original turns where possible rather than summarizing summaries, and keep the extracted facts verbatim rather than paraphrased.<br><br><strong>Measure it:</strong> build a multi-turn eval that plants a fact at turn 2 and asks about it at turn 40. Without that test you cannot tell whether your memory system works.` },

{ q: 'Your chatbot fails when users switch topics mid-conversation. How do you handle that?', tags: [], a: `The failure is that stale context dominates. Prior turns are in the prompt, attention weights them heavily, and the model keeps answering the old question — or fuses the two topics into something incoherent.<br><br><strong>Detect the switch.</strong> Cheapest reliable signal is embedding distance between the new message and the recent conversation centroid; a sharp drop indicates a shift. An LLM classifier ("is this a continuation or a new topic?") is more accurate and costs a small extra call. Explicit markers ("actually, different question") are strong signals worth pattern-matching.<br><br><strong>Then segment rather than reset.</strong> Do not clear history — users switch back, and losing it is a worse failure. Maintain topic segments and retrieve the relevant one: keep the new topic's turns in the active window, keep prior segments summarized and retrievable, and always keep the durable user-fact store in scope since preferences cross topics.<br><br><strong>For retrieval-backed bots, this is also a query-rewriting problem:</strong> a follow-up like "what about the other one?" needs the prior turn to be resolvable, but a genuine topic switch must <em>not</em> be contaminated by it. Condition the rewriter on the detected segment, not on raw history — otherwise topic switches produce retrieval queries polluted with the old subject, and the retrieval failure looks like a model failure.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> over-eager switch detection is worse than none, since it fragments coherent conversations. Tune the threshold on real transcripts and bias toward false negatives.` },

{ q: 'Your QA system always answers even when no answer exists. How do you detect unanswerable questions?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Handle it at multiple layers, because a single check will not hold.<br><br><strong>Before generation — retrieval confidence.</strong> If the best reranker score is below a calibrated threshold, refuse without calling the model at all. This is the cheapest and most reliable gate. Use the cross-encoder reranker score, not raw embedding cosine similarity, which is poorly calibrated and compresses into a narrow band.<br><br><strong>At generation — grounded prompting.</strong> Instruct the model to answer only from the provided context and emit an explicit marker (e.g. <code>INSUFFICIENT_CONTEXT</code>) otherwise, with few-shot examples that <em>demonstrate</em> abstaining. Without demonstrations the instruction is largely ignored, because the model's prior is overwhelmingly to answer.<br><br><strong>After generation — verification.</strong> Run an NLI/entailment check on whether each claim is supported by the retrieved passages, or require inline citations and programmatically verify every cited span exists. Unsupported claims trigger a refusal.<br><br><strong>Also fix the training signal if you fine-tune:</strong> SQuAD 2.0-style data with explicitly unanswerable examples teaches abstention directly; models trained only on answerable questions have never seen "no answer" as a valid target.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff to state:</strong> every threshold trades false refusals against hallucinations. Pick the operating point from the cost asymmetry in your domain — in medical or legal, a wrong answer costs far more than a refusal; in casual search, the reverse. Report both rates, not just one.` },

{ q: 'Your summarizer hallucinated facts not in the source. How do you fix it?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Distinguish the two failure types first, because the fixes differ. <strong>Extrinsic</strong> hallucination introduces facts absent from the source (usually parametric knowledge bleeding in). <strong>Intrinsic</strong> hallucination misstates or wrongly combines facts that <em>are</em> in the source — often caused by chunking that separated a claim from its qualifier.<br><br><strong>Reduce generation-time risk:</strong> temperature to 0 (sampling diversity is pure downside here), explicit grounding instructions, and for long documents ensure chunks preserve semantic boundaries — a summary that merges facts across a bad split is a chunking bug wearing a hallucination costume.<br><br><strong>Add verification, which is the real fix:</strong> require sentence-level attribution — every summary sentence cites source spans — then verify entailment with an NLI model or an LLM judge scoring each claim against its cited span. Unsupported sentences get regenerated or dropped. Automated metrics like AlignScore, SummaC, or QAFactEval operationalize this.<br><br><strong>Measure it properly:</strong> ROUGE cannot detect hallucination — it rewards n-gram overlap, and a fluent fabrication can score well. Teams that report ROUGE and ship hallucinating summarizers are measuring the wrong thing entirely. Use faithfulness metrics plus human spot-checks on a fixed eval set.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> map-reduce over long documents compounds this — an error introduced in a chunk summary is treated as ground truth by the reduce step, with no access to the source to catch it. Verify at the chunk level, before reduction.` },

{ q: 'Your generation repeats phrases in long outputs. How do you fix repetition?', tags: [], a: `Repetition is a known pathology of likelihood-trained models: repeated text is self-reinforcing, since having just said a phrase raises its probability of recurring, creating an attractor the decoder falls into. It is much worse under greedy/beam search than under sampling.<br><br><strong>Decoding fixes, in order:</strong> introduce sampling (top-p ~0.9 with temperature ~0.8) rather than pure greedy — this alone resolves most cases. Add a <code>repetition_penalty</code> (~1.1) or <code>frequency_penalty</code>, which downweight logits of already-emitted tokens; and <code>no_repeat_ngram_size</code> as a hard block on repeating any n-gram.<br><br><strong>Use them carefully.</strong> Blanket repetition penalties are blunt instruments — in code, structured output, or any text with legitimate recurring tokens (variable names, JSON keys, a repeated subject), they actively damage output. Prefer sampling adjustments first and reach for penalties only when needed, tuned per task.<br><br><strong>If it persists, look upstream:</strong> degeneration often signals the model has run out of things to say — the prompt underspecifies the task, or the requested length exceeds the available content. The fix is a better-scoped prompt or a shorter target, not a harder penalty. Repetition at the end of a long generation is usually a length problem, not a decoding problem.<br><br><strong>Also check the chat template.</strong> Malformed role markers or a missing EOS token cause loop-like output that looks like degeneration but is a formatting bug — verify the raw prompt string before tuning sampling.` },

{ q: 'Transformers work on text — can they also process images?', tags: [], a: `Yes, and the reason is that the Transformer has no text-specific machinery. Attention operates on a sequence of vectors; nothing in it assumes language. Anything you can turn into a sequence of embeddings is fair game.<br><br><strong>Vision Transformer (ViT)</strong> splits an image into fixed patches (e.g. 16×16), linearly projects each into a vector, adds positional embeddings, and feeds the sequence to a standard Transformer encoder. Patches are the tokens.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff versus CNNs:</strong> ViTs discard the convolutional inductive biases of locality and translation equivariance. That means they underperform CNNs on small datasets — they must <em>learn</em> what convolutions assume — but they scale better with data and compute, and they capture global relationships in the first layer rather than after many stacked receptive fields. With enough data (or strong augmentation and distillation, as in DeiT), they win.<br><br><strong>How this shows up in modern multimodal LLMs:</strong> a vision encoder produces patch embeddings, a projection layer maps them into the language model's embedding space, and they are passed as tokens alongside text — LLaVA-style. The LLM needs no architectural change, which is exactly the point: the sequence-of-vectors interface is what makes multimodality tractable.` },

{ q: 'What are Small Language Models (SLMs)?', tags: [], a: `SLMs are language models small enough to run under tight resource constraints — roughly 1B–15B parameters, and often on a single consumer GPU, a laptop, or a phone. The boundary is defined by deployment feasibility rather than a fixed parameter count.<br><br>They matter because the frontier-model default is frequently the wrong engineering choice. For a bounded task — classification, extraction, routing, structured transformation, domain QA — a fine-tuned 7B model regularly matches or beats a frontier model at a fraction of the cost and latency, with the added benefits of on-device/on-prem execution, data never leaving your boundary, and no per-token pricing.<br><br>The capability jump came from better data rather than architecture: Phi-style work showed that curated, textbook-quality and synthetic training data buys far more per parameter than scraped web text, and distillation from larger teachers transfers a lot of behavior.<br><br><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> SLMs degrade on broad world knowledge, multi-step reasoning, and long-context tasks. The right pattern is usually a hybrid — route the bulk of traffic to a small fine-tuned model and escalate genuinely hard queries to a frontier model, with a router or confidence gate deciding. That gets most of the cost saving without capping quality on the hard tail.` },

{ q: 'What are Large Reasoning Models (LRMs)?', tags: [], a: `LRMs are models trained to spend substantial inference-time compute on an internal reasoning process before answering — o1/o3, DeepSeek-R1, and similar. Rather than emitting an answer in one pass, they generate a long chain of thought, explore, backtrack, and self-correct, then produce a final response.<br><br>The training signal is the key change: instead of imitating human-written reasoning, these models are trained with RL against <em>verifiable</em> outcomes — math answers that check, code that passes tests. That lets the model discover reasoning strategies rather than copy them, and DeepSeek-R1 showed behaviors like self-verification and backtracking emerge from outcome rewards alone.<br><br><strong>The shift this represents:</strong> a second scaling axis. Previously more capability meant more pretraining compute; now you can also buy capability with more inference compute — and the two trade off, since a smaller model thinking longer can beat a larger model answering immediately.<br><br><strong>The costs a strong answer names:</strong> latency and token cost rise by 10–100x, making them unsuitable for interactive or high-volume paths; the gains concentrate in verifiable domains (math, code, logic) and are much weaker in open-ended writing; and reasoning traces are often hidden or summarized, which complicates debugging. The practical pattern is routing — use an LRM only for queries where the reasoning premium actually pays.` },

{ q: 'What are autoregressive models?', tags: [], a: `An autoregressive model factorizes the joint probability of a sequence into a product of conditionals via the chain rule: <code>P(x₁…xₙ) = Π P(xᵢ | x₁…xᵢ₋₁)</code>. Each element is predicted from all previous ones, so generation is inherently sequential.<br><br>This factorization is exact — no approximation — which is why it produces well-calibrated likelihoods and clean maximum-likelihood training. It is also self-supervised: any text is training data, since the label for each position is simply the next token. That property is what made pretraining scale.<br><br><strong>The structural cost:</strong> generation cannot be parallelized across positions. Training parallelizes perfectly (teacher forcing plus causal masking gives all positions in one pass), but inference must produce token t before t+1, which is why decode is latency-bound and why speculative decoding — draft cheaply, verify in parallel — exists.<br><br><strong>Second-order issue worth raising:</strong> exposure bias. During training the model always conditions on ground-truth prefixes; at inference it conditions on its own outputs, so an early error compounds down the sequence with no mechanism to revise. That is the theoretical root of degeneration and of why reasoning models benefit from being able to backtrack in their trace.` },

{ q: 'Explain the difference between autoregressive and masked language modeling.', tags: ['FREQUENTLY ASKED'], a: `<strong>Autoregressive (causal) LM</strong> predicts the next token given only the left context, trained with a causal mask. Every position is a training example, and the model can generate.<br><br><strong>Masked LM</strong> (BERT) randomly masks ~15% of tokens and predicts them from <em>bidirectional</em> context. Each position sees both directions, which produces richer representations for understanding tasks — but only the masked positions contribute loss, so it is roughly 6–7x less sample-efficient per pass, and the model cannot generate autoregressively.<br><br><strong>The mismatch worth naming:</strong> MLM has a train/inference discrepancy — <code>[MASK]</code> tokens appear during training but never at inference, which is why BERT uses the 80/10/10 replacement scheme to soften it.<br><br><strong>Why AR won for general LLMs:</strong> it unifies pretraining and the downstream interface. Any task can be phrased as text continuation, so the same objective that trains the model also serves it — no task-specific head, no fine-tuning required for a new task. MLM produces better <em>encoders</em>, which is why BERT-family models remain the right choice for embeddings, rerankers, and classification, but AR produces better general-purpose <em>systems</em>.` },

{ q: 'Explain Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO).', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `PPO is the policy-gradient algorithm used in the RL stage of classic RLHF. The problem it solves: vanilla policy gradients are unstable because a single large update can move the policy somewhere much worse, and the collected trajectories are then off-policy and useless.<br><br>PPO constrains each update with a <strong>clipped surrogate objective</strong> — it computes the probability ratio between new and old policy, and clips it to [1−ε, 1+ε] so that updates yielding ratios beyond that range get no additional gradient benefit. This bounds how far the policy can move per step without needing the expensive second-order machinery of TRPO.<br><br><strong>In the RLHF setting</strong> the pipeline needs four models in memory: the policy, a frozen reference policy (for the KL penalty that keeps outputs from drifting off-distribution), the reward model, and a value/critic model for advantage estimation. That memory and orchestration cost is PPO's main practical drawback.<br><br><strong>The honest assessment:</strong> PPO works and produced the first strong RLHF models, but it is finicky — sensitive to KL coefficient, learning rate, and reward normalization, and prone to reward hacking. That complexity is exactly why DPO gained adoption: it reaches similar quality on preference data without the reward model, the critic, or the RL loop. PPO retains an edge where you have a genuine online reward signal, especially verifiable rewards in math and code.` },

{ q: 'Explain Direct Preference Optimization (DPO).', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `DPO removes the reward model and the RL loop from preference tuning. The insight is mathematical: for the KL-constrained RLHF objective, the optimal policy has a closed-form relationship to the reward, so the reward can be expressed <em>in terms of</em> the policy and reference policy. Substituting that into the Bradley-Terry preference likelihood yields a simple classification-style loss directly on (prompt, chosen, rejected) triples.<br><br>Practically, you run supervised-style training that increases the log-probability margin between the chosen and rejected response relative to the frozen reference model. Two models in memory instead of four, no reward model to train, no sampling loop, no PPO hyperparameter tuning.<br><br><strong>Why it matters:</strong> it made preference alignment accessible — DPO is why most open fine-tunes ship aligned versions. Comparable quality to PPO on standard preference benchmarks at a fraction of the complexity.<br><br><strong>The limitations to state:</strong> DPO is <em>offline</em> — it trains on a fixed preference dataset, so it cannot explore or improve beyond that distribution the way online RL can. It is sensitive to the β (KL) parameter, and a known failure mode is that it can reduce the probability of chosen responses too (it optimizes the <em>margin</em>, not absolute quality), which motivated variants like IPO, KTO, and ORPO. Where you have a verifiable reward — math, code — online RL still wins.` },

{ q: 'Explain Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO).', tags: [], a: `GRPO is the RL algorithm behind DeepSeek-R1's reasoning training. Its core simplification: <strong>eliminate the value model</strong>. PPO needs a learned critic to estimate the baseline for advantage computation, which roughly doubles memory and adds its own training instability.<br><br>GRPO instead samples a <em>group</em> of G completions for the same prompt, scores them all, and computes each completion's advantage as its reward normalized against the group's mean and standard deviation. The group itself provides the baseline — a Monte Carlo estimate rather than a learned one. It keeps PPO's clipped objective and the KL penalty against a reference policy.<br><br><strong>Why this fits reasoning tasks specifically:</strong> for math and code you have cheap verifiable rewards (does the answer check, do the tests pass), and sampling multiple completions per prompt is natural. Group-relative scoring answers exactly the right question — "which of these attempts was better than typical for this problem" — without needing a critic to learn absolute value estimates, which is hard when rewards are sparse and terminal.<br><br><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> you pay G forward passes per prompt instead of one, so sampling cost is higher; the advantage estimate is noisier when G is small; and it depends on having a reliable reward function, so it transfers poorly to open-ended subjective tasks where DPO or PPO with a learned reward model remain more appropriate.` },

{ q: 'What are Recursive Language Models (RLMs)?', tags: [], a: `RLMs treat inference as a recursive decomposition rather than a single forward pass: the model can call <em>itself</em> on sub-parts of a problem, with each sub-call operating on a bounded context, and then combine the results. The root call orchestrates; leaf calls do local work.<br><br>The motivation is that a single pass over a very long context has both a quadratic cost and a well-documented attention degradation problem — "lost in the middle." Recursively decomposing lets each call see a manageable context it can actually attend over faithfully, so effective context becomes much larger than the nominal window and quality degrades more gracefully with input size.<br><br>Concretely this generalizes patterns already in production: map-reduce summarization, hierarchical document QA, and agents that spawn sub-agents with scoped context are all instances of the same idea. The research framing is treating the context as an environment the model can programmatically navigate and query rather than a flat string it must swallow whole.<br><br><strong>The costs:</strong> many model calls instead of one, so latency and token spend rise; error compounding across levels, since a bad sub-result is consumed as ground truth by the parent; and orchestration complexity in deciding how to split and how to merge. It is best viewed as trading inference compute and engineering complexity for effective context length — worth it when the input genuinely exceeds what one faithful pass can handle.` },

{ q: 'What is continual learning in LLMs?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Continual (lifelong) learning is updating a deployed model with new knowledge or capabilities over time without retraining from scratch. The central obstacle is <strong>catastrophic forgetting</strong>: gradient updates on new data overwrite weights encoding old capabilities, so a model fine-tuned on a new domain measurably degrades on everything else.<br><br><strong>Mitigations:</strong> <em>replay</em> — mix a sample of original training data into each update, the simplest and most effective approach; <em>regularization</em> — EWC and similar penalize changes to weights identified as important for prior tasks; <em>parameter isolation</em> — LoRA adapters per domain, leaving base weights frozen so nothing is overwritten and adapters can be composed or swapped.<br><br><strong>The pragmatic answer for most production systems is to avoid the problem:</strong> put changing knowledge in a retrieval store rather than in weights. RAG gives instant updates, deletion for real (a compliance requirement weights cannot satisfy), per-user access control, and source attribution — none of which continual training provides. Reserve weight updates for <em>capabilities and style</em>, which do not change often; use retrieval for <em>facts</em>, which do.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> continual training also breaks your evaluation baseline. Every update invalidates prior eval results, so without a regression suite that runs on every update you will ship silent degradation — and forgetting is invisible until a user hits the forgotten capability.` },

{ q: 'How do Diffusion Language Models (DLMs) work?', tags: [], a: `Diffusion LMs generate text by iterative denoising rather than left-to-right sampling. Training corrupts a sequence — masking tokens or adding noise in a continuous embedding space — and the model learns to reverse the corruption. Generation starts from a fully corrupted sequence and refines <em>all positions in parallel</em> over a fixed number of denoising steps.<br><br><strong>The structural advantages over autoregression:</strong> generation is parallel across positions rather than strictly sequential, so latency scales with the number of denoising steps (a tunable constant) instead of output length — potentially a large throughput win for long outputs. The model also sees the full sequence bidirectionally at every step, so it can revise earlier tokens in light of later ones, which autoregressive models structurally cannot do. That makes them a natural fit for infilling and for global constraint satisfaction, and it sidesteps exposure bias.<br><br><strong>Why they have not displaced AR models:</strong> discrete text is a poor fit for a formulation designed for continuous data, so the discrete-diffusion formulations are less mature. Quality per unit compute still trails AR models at scale, the KV-cache trick that makes AR decoding cheap has no clean analogue, and the entire tooling ecosystem assumes autoregression. Recent work (LLaDA, Gemini Diffusion, Mercury) has narrowed the gap enough to make this a live research direction rather than a curiosity — the compelling claim is order-of-magnitude faster generation at competitive quality.` },

{ q: 'How does LLM watermarking work?', tags: [], a: `Watermarking embeds a statistically detectable signal into generated text without perceptibly changing its quality. The dominant approach (Kirchenbauer et al.) works at the decoding step: hash the previous token(s) with a secret key to pseudorandomly split the vocabulary into a "green list" and a "red list," then add a small bias δ to green-list logits.<br><br>Because the split is deterministic given the key and the preceding context, a detector with the key can recompute which tokens should have been green and count how many actually are. Human text hits ~50% green by chance; watermarked text hits substantially more, and the deviation is testable with a z-statistic — giving a detection decision with a computable p-value, which is what makes it defensible compared to classifier-based AI-detectors.<br><br><strong>The tradeoffs:</strong> stronger bias means more reliable detection but more quality distortion; detection needs enough tokens (typically a few hundred) so short outputs are undetectable; and low-entropy text — code, quotations, factual answers with one correct phrasing — cannot be watermarked without breaking correctness, since there is no freedom to shift token choice.<br><br><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> watermarks are removable. Paraphrasing through another model, translation round-trips, or heavy editing degrade the signal, and an open-weights model can simply have watermarking disabled. It is a useful provenance signal for cooperative, at-scale use — platform-level content labeling — not a security control against a motivated adversary. Also note the false-positive cost: accusing a human of AI generation on a statistical test is a serious error, so thresholds must be set conservatively.` },

{ q: 'How do RNNs and Transformers differ?', tags: ['FREQUENTLY ASKED'], a: `<strong>RNNs</strong> process tokens sequentially, carrying a fixed-size hidden state forward. Training cannot parallelize across time steps, and information from distant tokens must survive many multiplicative updates — the vanishing-gradient problem LSTMs and GRUs mitigate with gating but do not eliminate. The path length between two tokens is O(distance).<br><br><strong>Transformers</strong> replace recurrence with attention: every token attends to every other in one operation. Path length is O(1) regardless of distance, and the whole sequence trains in parallel, which is what let them absorb GPU scale.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff is precise:</strong> RNNs are O(n) time and O(1) memory per step at inference but cannot parallelize training. Transformers parallelize training perfectly but cost O(n²) attention and require a KV cache that grows linearly with context at inference. RNNs compress history lossily into a fixed state; Transformers keep everything and pay for it.<br><br><strong>Why this question is live again:</strong> modern state-space models — Mamba, RWKV — are essentially RNNs redesigned to be parallelizable during training (via associative scans) while keeping constant-memory inference. They recover the RNN's inference advantage without its training penalty. The remaining gap is precise recall: attention can look up any past token exactly, while a compressed recurrent state cannot — which is why hybrid architectures interleaving attention and SSM layers are the current pragmatic answer.` },
]};
